  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Gulab Prasad vs The State Of Bihar on 18 December, 2018",
    "facts": "Prosecution Case in short is that complainant (PW- 2), namely, Md. Iqbal, along with his Peon, inspected the shop of the petitioner and introduced himself as Food Inspector and he found Haldi powder suspected to be adulterated and purchased 450 Grams of Haldi powder on payment for chemical examination as sample. It is said that the sample was equally divided into three parts. Each part of sample was labeled and wrapped with thick papers and packed and sealed as per provisions of the PFA Act and on next succeeding day, one part of the sample has been sent to Public Analyst, Government of Bihar, Agamkuan, Patna. It further appears that the report submitted by the local health authority/Public Analyst declaring that Gulab Prasad vs The State Of Bihar on 18 December, 2018 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/137096997/ 1 sample had presence of lead chromate which renders it injurious to health. 3. The prosecution report has been submitted to the learned trial court and two witnesses were examined on behalf of the prosecution i.e., PW-1 Ravindra Nath Choubey (Peon) and PW-2 Md. Iqbal (Food Inspector). On behalf of the defence, no witness was examined and he claimed to be innocence and of false implication. 4. Being aggrieved by the aforesaid order, the present revision application has been preferred.",
